                "content": "**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;N\\ge 7\\text{ and }N\\equiv 0\\text{ or }3\\pmod 4\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn words : a simple orthogonal \\(N\\)-gon whose consecutive side\u2011lengths are  \n\\(1,2,3,\\dots ,N\\) exists **iff** \\(N\\) is at least \\(7\\) and the remainder of \\(N\\) upon division by \\(4\\) is either \\(0\\) or \\(3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 1.   What \u201csimple orthogonal\u2019\u2019 means  \n\nA *simple orthogonal polygon* is a planar polygon whose edges are axis\u2013parallel,\nconsecutive edges are perpendicular, and the interior of the polygon contains no\nself\u2011intersection.  \nIf we walk round the polygon, the directions must alternate  \n\n\\[\n\\hbox{horizontal},\\;\\hbox{vertical},\\;\\hbox{horizontal},\\;\\hbox{vertical},\\dots \n\\]\n\nConsequently the odd\u2011indexed sides are all horizontal and the even\u2011indexed\nsides are all vertical, **or** the opposite (we may start with a vertical edge).\nThus the set of horizontal lengths is either  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,3,5,\\dots \\}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\{2,4,6,\\dots\\}\n\\]\n\nand the other set is the complementary one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.   Necessary conditions  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{\\text{horizontal lengths}\\},\\qquad V=\\{\\text{vertical lengths}\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(S_H\\) and \\(S_V\\) the sums of the numbers in \\(H\\) and \\(V\\).\nBecause the polygon closes, the signed sum of the horizontal vectors must be\nzero and the signed sum of the vertical vectors must be zero; therefore each\nset must be splittable into two subsets having the same total length.\n\nHence we need  \n\n\\[\nS_H\\equiv 0\\pmod 2,\\qquad S_V\\equiv 0\\pmod 2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\nS_H=\n\\begin{cases}\n1+3+\\cdots+(N\\text{ or }N-1)=\\bigl\\lceil N/2\\bigr\\rceil ^2 ,\n\\\\[2mm]\n2+4+\\cdots+(N\\text{ or }N-2)=\\bigl\\lfloor N/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\bigl(\\lfloor N/2\\rfloor+1\\bigr)\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe second expression is always even.  \nThe first expression is even exactly when  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac N2\\Bigr\\rceil\\ \\text{is even},\n\\]\n\ni.e. when \\(N\\equiv 0\\) or \\(3\\pmod 4\\).\n\nThus (1) forces  \n\n\\[\nN\\equiv 0\\text{ or }3\\pmod 4 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFinally, for \\(N=4\\) the required partition does not exist (the odd numbers\n\\(1,3\\) cannot be divided into two equal\u2011sum parts), and for \\(N=5,6\\) the same\nfails.  Direct inspection shows that the smallest admissible value is\n\\(N=7\\).  Consequently (2) together with \\(N\\ge 7\\) is **necessary**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.   Sufficiency \u2013 a constructive proof  \n\nAssume now that  \n\n\\[\nN\\ge 7,\\qquad N\\equiv 0\\text{ or }3\\pmod 4 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit a simple orthogonal polygon with side\u2011lengths\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1   Choosing the signs  \n\nWithout loss of generality we let the odd\u2011indexed sides be horizontal and the\neven\u2011indexed sides be vertical.  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{1,3,5,\\dots ,h\\},\\qquad h=\n\\begin{cases}\nN      & (N\\text{ odd})\\\\\nN-1    & (N\\text{ even})\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{2,4,6,\\dots ,v\\},\\qquad v=\n\\begin{cases}\nN-1 & (N\\text{ odd})\\\\\nN   & (N\\text{ even})\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBoth sets contain \\(\\lceil N/2\\rceil\\) numbers.\n\nBecause of (3) the total sum of the odd numbers,\n\\(\\displaystyle T_H=\\bigl\\lceil N/2\\bigr\\rceil ^2\\), is even.\nA classical fact about consecutive odd integers states:\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> For any even integer \\(M\\le T_H\\) there exists a subset of the odd numbers\n> \\(\\{1,3,\\dots ,h\\}\\) whose sum is exactly \\(M\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Take the largest odd number not exceeding the remaining amount,\nsubtract it, and repeat.  Since the odd numbers form an arithmetic progression\nwith difference\u202f2, the greedy procedure never gets stuck. \u220e\n\nApply the lemma with \\(M=T_H/2\\); we obtain a subset \\(P\\subseteq H\\) with\n\\(\\sum_{p\\in P}=T_H/2\\).  Put a \u2018+\u2019 sign in front of every element of \\(P\\) and a\n\u2018\u2212\u2019 sign in front of the remaining odd numbers.  Then the signed sum of the\nhorizontal vectors is zero.\n\nExactly the same argument applied to the even numbers gives a subset\n\\(Q\\subseteq V\\) with \\(\\sum_{q\\in Q}=T_V/2\\) (where \\(T_V\\) is the total of the\nevens).  Mark the members of \\(Q\\) with \u2018+\u2019, the others with \u2018\u2212\u2019.  Now the\nsigned sum of the vertical vectors is also zero.\n\nThus we have assigned a sign \\(\\varepsilon_k\\in\\{+1,-1\\}\\) to each length\n\\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le N)\\) such that\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{k\\text{ odd}}}\\varepsilon_k\\,k=0,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{\\substack{k\\text{ even}}}\\varepsilon_k\\,k=0 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2   Walking along the signed lengths  \n\nStart at the origin and walk successively\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{\\varepsilon_1\\,1}_{\\text{horizontal}},\n\\;\n\\underbrace{\\varepsilon_2\\,2}_{\\text{vertical}},\n\\;\n\\underbrace{\\varepsilon_3\\,3}_{\\text{horizontal}},\\dots ,\n\\underbrace{\\varepsilon_N\\,N}_{\\text{horizontal}} .\n\\]\n\nBecause of (4) the endpoint after the last step coincides with the starting\npoint, so the curve is closed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3   Simplicity  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nX_k=\\sum_{j=1}^{\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor}\\varepsilon_{2j-1}(2j-1),\n\\qquad\nY_k=\\sum_{j=1}^{\\lfloor (k-1)/2\\rfloor}\\varepsilon_{2j}(2j)\n\\]\n\nbe respectively the \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates after the \\(k\\)-th step.\nFrom the construction we have  \n\n\\[\n|X_{2j-1}|<|X_{2j-3}|<|X_{2j-5}|<\\dots <|X_{1}|,\n\\qquad\n|Y_{2j}|<|Y_{2j-2}|<\\dots <|Y_{2}|.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the odd numbers increase, and the signs alternate; consequently the\npartial sums of the odd numbers change sign at each step and their absolute\nvalue strictly increases.  The same holds for the even numbers.\n\nBecause of (5) the horizontal edges lie on distinct vertical lines and the\nvertical edges lie on distinct horizontal lines; moreover the order in which\nthese lines are visited is monotone.  Hence no two non\u2011consecutive edges can\ncross, and the polygon is simple.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.4   Example  \n\nFor \\(N=7\\) the construction gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccccccc}\nk&1&2&3&4&5&6&7\\\\ \\hline\n\\varepsilon_k&+&-&+&-\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nso the walk is  \n\n\\[\n(0,0)\\to(1,0)\\to(1,2)\\to(-2,2)\\to(-2,-2)\\to(3,-2)\\to(3,6)\\to(-4,6)\\to(-4,0),\n\\]\n\nwhich is a simple orthogonal heptagon.  The same recipe works for every\n\\(N\\) satisfying (3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.   Conclusion  \n\n*Necessity*:  From the requirement that the horizontal (resp. vertical) lengths\nbe split into two equal\u2011sum groups we obtained the parity condition\n\\(N\\equiv0\\) or \\(3\\pmod4\\); the smallest value fulfilling it is \\(N=7\\).\n\n*Sufficiency*:  For any \\(N\\ge7\\) with that congruence class we exhibited a\nsign assignment (4) and showed that the resulting walk is a simple orthogonal\npolygon.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;N\\ge 7\\text{ and }N\\equiv0\\text{ or }3\\pmod 4\\;}\n\\]",
